[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, rule-slot conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve pile roles, card flow, move graph, and exact card totals where possible. A runnable near-neighbor reveals far more than a speculative redesign layered on top of a broken file.\n- Re-check rule/setup compatibility after every structural fix. Nonstandard deck composition, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, and engine-specific rule contexts can make familiar-looking move or win rules invalid, unreachable, or misleading.\n- If missing cards are restored or new piles are introduced during repair, make sure those cards still participate in both movement and victory; otherwise you replace a compile problem with a structurally unwinnable game.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine effectively allows one rule per source/destination pair, merge exceptions into one coherent condition tree instead of splitting behavior across competing moves.\n\n## Align sinks, victory, and deck structure\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can absorb the entire deck under the actual suit/copy structure. Rearrangement alone is not a completion plan.\n- Size sink capacity and parallelism to the deck\u2019s true completion units, not to standard 52-card conventions. Reduced-suit, duplicated-suit, and multi-copy decks often need different numbers of foundations or different sink structures entirely.\n- Match sink entry rules to ordinary play. Prefer sinks that accept progress in small, common increments; sinks that only fit rare preassembled patterns, only accept fresh entries, or only score in all-at-once bursts usually create churn instead of wins.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, fix sink mismatch before adding mobility. Broadening sink compatibility, changing the rewarded completion pattern, or repurposing an existing one-way pile into the main victory sink is usually more effective than making the tableau looser.\n- Keep one-way piles purposeful. If a receiving area is not itself a rewarded completion sink, give it a reliable release route; otherwise it becomes dead storage or search noise.\n- Avoid overly permissive sinks. A pile that accepts almost anything without meaningful sequencing stops being a progress sink and becomes generic storage, which can preserve win rate while collapsing decisions and pile roles.\n- Align the win condition to actual completion. Track the piles that represent irreversible progress, and make sure every participating pile type has a legal path into that objective.\n\n## Shape mobility to support progress\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Very short exhausted losses usually indicate blocked opening contact or structural impossibility; long high-usage losses and search-cap failures usually indicate reversible motion without enough rewarded progress.\n- Favor movement rules that build in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau and stack moves are most useful when they convert exposure into sink-ready order instead of supporting free circulation disconnected from victory.\n- When wins already exist but many losses are long, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding new freedom. Unrestricted empty fills, oversized stack transfers, unlimited redeals, and loose helper piles are common churn sources.\n- Prefer constrained workspace over unrestricted parking. Rank-gated empties, single-card access, or stack rules that mirror ordinary building usually preserve planning value while reducing solver noise.\n- If mobility is high but progress is shallow, consider adding or tightening structured stack moves rather than more generic single-card freedom. Movement that preserves meaningful order can reduce churn while improving real decision-making.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small changes\n- When losses are exhausted in 0\u20134 moves, first check for a real win path; if the sink is valid, then treat the problem as opening contact. Small increases in starting exposure often rescue dead starts more cleanly than broadening all movement rules.\n- Use narrow workspace fixes before broad mobility buffs. A little more initial information, a modest increase in tableau capacity, or limited single-card access to empties can unblock openings without flattening the game.\n- If some deals already win but many others die immediately, suspect cramped starts more than flawed endgame structure. Improve fairness across shuffles with the smallest exposure or workspace change that preserves the core flow.\n- When changing tableau count or stock/deal behavior, remember that pile count also controls pacing, congestion, and how quickly cards enter active play. Count-preserving setup changes can fix both opening contact and deal rhythm at once.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a solitaire shows long, high-usage losses with some deals proven impossible, the problem is often not mobility but lack of a realistic irreversible sink. Adding or repurposing a receiving pile so that ordinary tableau patterns can flow into it is often more effective than further loosening movement rules.\n2. When a solitaire wins on every shuffle while still using nearly all cards and piles, the problem is often not excess mobility alone but an over-permissive completion sink. Tightening the sink to reward more specific progress is usually a better next step than adding more obstacles elsewhere, because it preserves activity while restoring meaningful decisions.\n3. When a solitaire file fails deck accounting, repair it with the smallest count-preserving change that keeps existing pile roles intact; getting back to a runnable near-neighbor is more informative than redesigning around a compile error.\n4. When a solitaire already has occasional wins but most losses end at the search cap with high pile/card usage, the usual problem is churn, not pure deadness. Adding a structured tableau-to-tableau stack move that preserves the same ordering the sink rewards can improve meaningful progress more effectively than adding more generic storage or draw access.\n5. When a game shows long, high-usage losses with no wins, suspect a progress-sink mismatch before adding more mobility. If the victory piles cannot legally absorb the full deck under the actual deck multiplicity and suit set, the solver will churn forever no matter how many tableau moves exist.\n6. When losses exhaust in just a few moves across every shuffle, first look for a missing completion path rather than insufficient mobility. If the only irreversible sink accepts just a small subset of cards, either broaden that sink into a true end-state or change victory to match what the game can actually complete.\n7. When a solitaire asks tableau piles to empty, first verify that the irreversible sink can actually absorb the whole deck under its own entry rules. A near-miss in sink capacity or sequence length creates provably unwinnable deals no amount of extra mobility can repair.\n8. When a solitaire has long non-exhaustive losses with moderate card usage, suspect \u201cprogressless mobility\u201d: cards can move, but not into a sink that actually satisfies victory. Converting an existing one-way holding area into a true completion target is often a more effective fix than adding yet more movement freedom.\n9. When a solitaire has plenty of legal rearrangement but zero wins, check whether the victory sink matches the deck\u2019s actual completion units. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, requiring every sink pile to build from Ace to King can be much harder than it looks; a sink that accepts common, naturally assembled sequences in the same direction as tableau play often fixes structural unwinnability more effectively than adding more mobility.\n10. When a solitaire already produces substantial card/pile usage and some wins, but many losses stall at search limits instead of exhausting quickly, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of access. In that situation, a small change to how empty spaces are reused can improve practical solvability and decision quality more effectively than broadly loosening all movement rules.\n11. When a compile error comes from a victory rule, prefer the smallest repair that keeps the existing card flow intact. In solitaire, a win condition should usually track the real irreversible sinks and active holding areas; if cards can only leave the stock through those areas, emptying them often already implies the stock was consumed.\n12. When a solitaire file fails deck accounting, favor the smallest count-preserving repair that keeps pile roles and movement intact; getting to a runnable near-neighbor is more informative than redesigning around an invalid setup.\nFor multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, foundation/workspace counts that look \"standard\" can hide arithmetic mismatches\u2014always recompute total cards explicitly before tuning gameplay.\n13. When some deals already win but others are proven dead in just a few moves, prefer a small increase in starting exposure over broader mobility changes. Revealing a little more information in the deepest piles often fixes unwinnable openings without turning the whole tableau into free-form storage.\n14. When a solitaire shows high move counts and broad card usage but no wins, the issue is often not mobility but sink access: the game needs more frequent, irreversible ways to convert exposed cards into progress. In multi-deck designs, broadening the legal starting points of completion piles can unlock wins more effectively than making tableau movement even looser.\n15. When a pile type already behaves like the game\u2019s irreversible progress sink, make the win condition measure completion there instead of requiring unrelated holding piles to empty. In multi-copy decks, also check that sink rules can absorb all copies of a suit sequence rather than silently capping progress after the first complete run.\n16. When a reduced-suit deck contains duplicate copies of each suit, foundation count must scale to the number of complete suit sequences present, not to the number of distinct suits alone. If most deals are exhausted almost immediately, check whether the victory sink can physically absorb the whole deck before tuning movement freedom.\n17. When a solitaire already wins often but some losses are exhausted almost immediately, the problem is usually opening contact, not endgame structure. A small increase in starting information or exposure can remove dead deals without flattening the rest of the decision space.\n18. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but zero wins, check whether its irreversible receiving piles are actually the ones the win condition rewards. Converting a one-way storage area into the true completion sink is often more effective than adding even more mobility.\n19. When a solitaire file fails to compile because cards are missing, prefer the smallest count-preserving repair that keeps existing pile roles intact. Restoring a runnable near-neighbor is usually more informative than redesigning rules before you have any play data.\n20. When some deals are provably dead immediately but others already win, first improve opening contact by aligning tableau-to-tableau building with the freedoms already present elsewhere in the game. Expanding local compatibility can rescue blocked starts more cleanly than loosening the scoring sink, which often increases churn instead of real progress.\n21. When a solitaire\u2019s losses are mostly long searches or immediate exhausted dead starts, first verify that the victory sink can actually absorb the whole deck under the deck\u2019s copy/suit structure. A sink rule that is mathematically impossible, or too few sink piles for the number of full sequences in the deck, creates unwinnability that mobility tweaks won\u2019t solve.\n22. When a solver explores deeply without finding wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but mobility in the wrong pattern. Make rearrangement rules help assemble cards in ways that expose or support the sink, rather than demanding the same rare structure both in the tableau and at the foundations.\n23. When a game\u2019s win condition requires tableau piles to empty, make sure there is a realistic irreversible sink that can absorb the whole deck in ordinary play. If losses are fully exhausted across all sampled deals, fixing sink reachability is usually more important than adding extra mobility.\n24. When a solitaire file fails before simulation, favor the smallest repair that restores a runnable core and preserves the intended card flow; meaningful balance tuning can only start once the sink, setup, and win condition can be tested in play.\n25. When a solitaire can rearrange lots of cards but never wins, the main issue is often the scoring sink, not tableau mobility. A completion area that only accepts large preassembled patterns tends to create search churn; switching to smaller, frequent, irreversible deposits can restore a real path to victory without rewriting the whole game.\n26. When a solitaire has long, search-cap losses with moderate card usage, suspect a sink mismatch before adding more mobility: the bot is often circulating cards without a realistic way to convert that motion into irreversible progress.\n\nIn multi-copy or duplicated-deck games, completion piles usually need compatibility tuned to the deck structure; requiring exact suit on foundations can make a nominal sink too narrow even when enough pile capacity exists.\n27. When a solitaire variant\u2019s win depends on irreversible completion, the receiving piles must have both enough total capacity for the full deck and legal access from every area that contributes cards. A compile-safe but unreachable win is often caused by sink mismatch, not by insufficient tableau mobility.\n28. When a game already has some wins but many losses end at the search limit, look for redundant reversible choices or duplicate sink destinations before adding more mobility. Extra empty receiving piles that exceed the deck\u2019s real completion units often increase solver churn more than they increase meaningful progress.\n29. When a solitaire requires tableau and helper piles to empty, first verify that the irreversible sink can actually absorb the full deck under the game\u2019s real suit/copy structure. If sink capacity only fits part of the deck, no amount of extra mobility will create wins; fix completion capacity before tuning difficulty.\n30. When a solitaire file fails basic deck accounting, favor the smallest structural repair that preserves the intended pile roles and total pacing. A runnable near-neighbor is much more informative than redesigning rules around a setup that never actually enters play.\n31. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search limits with moderate-to-high card usage but zero wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of a practical irreversible sink. Converting a \u201cperfect-run-only\u201d scoring pile into a foundation that accepts frequent incremental progress often improves both solvability and decision quality more than further loosening tableau movement.\n32. If a one-way receiving pile exists, it should usually be either the actual victory sink or have a reliable release path. Otherwise, any move into it becomes irreversible noise or a trap, and the game can become unwinnable even when ordinary tableau motion looks plausible.\n33. When a deck contains duplicated suits or multiple copies, foundation capacity must match the number of full suit sequences that actually exist. If the sink only has room for one run per nominal suit, the game can be provably unwinnable no matter how flexible the tableau is.\n34. When a solitaire already has a real completion sink but most losses are short, exhausted, and use almost no cards, improve opening information before broadening movement rules. Small exposure changes often raise fairness across shuffles without adding churn or weakening the game\u2019s core structure.\n35. When a solitaire shows long, high-usage losses with no wins, the problem is often not lack of movement but lack of a practical irreversible sink. Replacing an all-at-once completion rule with incremental scoring that matches ordinary card exposure can improve both solvability and decision quality more effectively than further loosening tableau movement.\n36. When a solitaire file fails to compile, the best next step is usually the smallest rules-legal repair that preserves the existing card flow and pile roles. A runnable near-neighbor is far more informative than a speculative balance pass on top of invalid rules.\n37. When a solitaire can rearrange cards freely but only scores progress in rare, fully preassembled chunks, the result is often long unwon searches rather than meaningful difficulty. Converting the sink to accept small, ordinary increments usually helps more than adding yet more tableau mobility.\n38. When a completion sink builds by exact-rank matching rather than by full-sequence advancement, its total number of pile slots can create hard unwinnability even if the move rules look generous. Fix sink capacity first; a small count-preserving reallocation from stock to scoring piles often improves fairness more cleanly than loosening tableau movement.\n39. When a game's main piles must become empty to win, there must be a distinct sink that can absorb the whole deck irreversibly under normal play. A helper pile that only stores cards temporarily is not enough; either give it a real completion-building role or align the win condition to a pile type that already represents irreversible progress.\n40. When a solitaire engine treats each source/destination pair as a single rule slot, split \u201cspecial case + normal case\u201d moves should be merged into one OR tree rather than written as separate moves. Minimal compile repairs that preserve pile roles are usually better than redesigning before you have any play data.\n41. If a solitaire has a valid sink but no way to rearrange blockers toward it, losses will be exhaustively proven even with plenty of stock cards left. Before tuning difficulty, make sure the tableau has at least one movement rule that exposes buried sink-eligible cards and that empty-space rules create planning value rather than total freedom.\n42. When a solitaire already shows full card/pile usage and occasional wins, but most losses hit search limits rather than exhausting quickly, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than lack of access. Tightening the broadest \u201cparking\u201d rule\u2014especially empty-pile fills\u2014can improve decision quality and reduce churn without changing the game\u2019s basic structure.\n43. When many deals are proven unwinnable after only a handful of moves, add opening contact before changing the whole ruleset. A small increase in starting workspace\u2014especially an extra empty or lighter tableau created by redistributing the same cards\u2014can improve fairness across shuffles without collapsing the game's core constraints.\n44. When some deals win cleanly but many others are exhausted with almost no card usage, the issue is often opening contact rather than the core move logic. In deal-based solitaire, a small increase in how many cards enter active play per draw can improve fairness across shuffles more effectively than broadening tableau rules.\n45. When a solitaire already wins sometimes but most losses hit search caps instead of exhausting quickly, the problem is often reversible churn rather than insufficient freedom. Tightening the broadest movement rule so it builds in the same pattern the sink rewards can reduce noise and improve meaningful progress without a full redesign.\n46. When repairing a broken solitaire file, prefer the smallest fix that preserves the existing card flow and pile roles. In particular, a deal-type stock is a distributor rather than a normal movable source, so treat it as setup/feeding infrastructure unless the rules engine explicitly supports direct moves from it.\n47. When some deals already win but other deals are proven unwinnable almost immediately, prefer small count-preserving setup changes that increase initial contact points. Adding one more exposed starting card or one more live pile often improves shuffle fairness more cleanly than loosening core move rules.\n48. If a solitaire asks the player to empty tableau piles, it needs a completion sink that can absorb the whole deck under ordinary play. Small \u201chelper\u201d piles that only accept rare starters or have too little capacity often