but zero wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of a practical conversion from rearrangement into irreversible progress. Adding a sink-compatible stack transfer can improve solvability more effectively than further loosening generic tableau movement.\n55. When a game has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, the first thing to check is whether the completion sink matches the deck\u2019s repetition structure and the way cards are naturally assembled. A sink that rewards full ordered runs can fit multi-copy single-suit decks better than many parallel ace-up foundations, because it converts tableau organization directly into irreversible progress instead of creating duplicate-rank traffic jams.\n56. When a solitaire already wins often but some deals are proven unwinnable almost immediately, the problem is often opening contact rather than the endgame sink. A small increase in initial exposure on just part of the tableau can improve shuffle fairness more cleanly than broadening movement rules across the whole game.\n57. When a solitaire shows only long, inconclusive losses, suspect reversible churn before adding even more mobility. A small reduction in stock recursion and a sink structure sized to the deck\u2019s actual completion units often improves solvability more than loosening tableau rules.\n58. When losses are exhausted in only a few moves, check for a true completion path before tuning mobility. Rearrangement plus a sink that only accepts partial sequences\u2014or a win condition that ignores the sink entirely\u2014creates provably dead games no matter how much opening freedom you add.\n59. When a win condition depends on fully building a set of sink piles, every sink must be legally startable and meaningfully reachable under the deck\u2019s actual copy count. Extra \u201cfoundation-like\u201d piles that cannot be opened or completed create search noise and pseudo-progress; converting them into explicit workspace often improves both clarity and solvability more than loosening general movement.\n60. When a solitaire already has some wins but many losses end at the search cap, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than lack of legal moves. Tightening stack movement on the noisiest helper piles can improve decision quality more effectively than adding more freedom everywhere.\n61. When a solitaire already has some wins but most losses hit a search cap, look first for the broadest reversible move rather than adding more freedom. Tightening empty-pile entry is often a high-leverage way to reduce churn while preserving the core sink and sequencing rules.\n62. When a solitaire already has occasional wins but most losses hit the search cap, the problem is often reversible storage rather than lack of mobility. Tightening the most permissive empty-fill rule can reduce churn while keeping the underlying completion path intact.\n63. When some deals are winnable but many others die in the opening with exhaustive proof, first smooth the starting layout before rewriting movement rules. Redistributing the same cards across more even tableau sizes can improve opening contact and fairness across shuffles without changing the game\u2019s core logic.\n64. When a game shows high card usage but mostly long, inconclusive losses, the problem is often reversible storage rather than lack of mobility. Converting a broad auxiliary pile from multi-card parking into limited single-card workspace can reduce churn while keeping meaningful tactical choices.\n65. When a solitaire file fails basic card accounting, fix that first with the smallest role-preserving adjustment you can. A runnable near-neighbor of the broken design gives much better feedback than a speculative redesign layered over an invalid setup.\n66. When a game uses multiple copies of the same suits, foundation count must match completion capacity, not just the presence of aces. A tableau can be very mobile and still be unwinnable if its only irreversible sink can absorb only one copy of each suit sequence.\n67. When a multi-deck game shows long, high-usage losses with no wins, the bottleneck is often the completion sink rather than mobility. In duplicated-suit decks, requiring exact-suit foundation building can make progress too narrow; loosening the sink to match a broader invariant already used elsewhere (such as color) can create real completion without turning the tableau into free storage.\n68. When many shuffled starts have zero or near-zero legal moves, the issue is often lack of opening contact rather than overall difficulty. Adding a structured stack-transfer rule that mirrors the game\u2019s existing build pattern can create early options and improve fairness across deals without resorting to broad unrestricted storage.\n69. When a design already has a nominal foundation sink, first verify that victory is actually aligned to using it. If wins require \u201cempty everything\u201d but the only irreversible progress area is treated as optional, the game often devolves into temporary shuffling without a clear completion path.\n70. When a solitaire shows long, high-usage losses with zero wins, the problem is often not insufficient mobility but an end condition that rewards only rare, all-at-once patterns. Converting progress into smaller irreversible steps that match common play sequences usually improves solvability more effectively than further loosening tableau movement.\n71. If a pile type only receives cards and has no exit, it should usually be part of irreversible progress, not something the win condition demands be emptied. Before tuning mobility, make sure every one-way destination either contributes directly to victory or has a legal release path.\n72. When a solitaire has no pile that can permanently absorb all cards needed by the win condition, most losses will be provably unwinnable no matter how much local mobility exists. Fix the completion path first: give one pile type a real sink role and align the win condition to the piles that are meant to empty into it.\n73. When a game already has a real completion sink but many deals are proven unwinnable after very short searches, the bottleneck is often opening contact rather than overall mobility. Increasing starting information can improve fairness across shuffles more cleanly than loosening movement rules or adding extra storage.\n74. When a solitaire already wins sometimes but many losses hit a search cap with high usage, the problem is often reversible tableau churn rather than lack of mobility. Tightening the broadest relocation rule\u2014especially empty-pile fills for whole stacks\u2014can improve decision quality without making the game structurally harsher.\n75. When a solitaire produces only long search-cap losses, look first for reversible traffic disconnected from victory. Converting an existing one-way holding area into a true completion sink, and sizing sink piles to the deck\u2019s actual completion units, often helps more than adding even more mobility.\n76. When a variant already wins often but has a minority of fully exhausted losses, first check whether those losses come from structural impossibility or from cramped openings. If the sink is already sufficient, a small increase in active pile count can improve shuffle fairness more cleanly than loosening all move rules.\n77. When repairing a non-running solitaire, prefer the smallest fix that restores the intended card flow rather than redesigning around the error. Especially with stock mechanics, first verify whether the draw pile is a movable source at all; a deal-only stock should usually influence play by feeding piles, not by acting like a hand or waste.\n78. When a variant already has occasional full-deck wins, prefer tuning the loosest reversible workspace before adding more mobility. Constraining how empty helper piles are filled can preserve their tactical value while reducing search churn and improving consistency across deals.\n79. When some shuffles already win cleanly but many others exhaust in the first few moves, prefer a small increase in initial exposure over a broad rules loosening. Improving early contact often raises fairness across deals while preserving the game's underlying structure and decision-making.\n80. When every tested deal is exhausted after only a handful of moves, suspect a missing card-flow path before tuning difficulty. In solitaire, a sink pile should usually be fed by ordinary play in irreversible chunks, while stock/deal behavior should avoid injecting cards directly into one-way completion piles unless that is itself the scoring mechanism.\n81. When a solver explores deeply without finding wins, the problem is often congestion rather than pure lack of legal moves: too few active tableau piles can create massive reversible churn. In dense stock-dealing games, redistributing the same starting cards across more piles is a compact way to improve exposure, workspace, and deal pacing without changing the core sink rules.\n82. When a solitaire produces only long, unresolved searches, suspect a mismatch between reversible movement and irreversible progress before adding more mobility. A small redesign that turns helper piles into true workspace and sizes the completion sinks to the deck\u2019s actual completion units often helps more than loosening tableau rules.\n83. When a solitaire already wins often and uses all of its components, but its losses are short and fully exhausted, the problem is often opening visibility rather than core progression. A small increase in starting face-up information can improve fairness across shuffles without flattening the game's structure.\n84. When a solitaire has long, high-usage losses with no wins, the main problem is often sink mismatch rather than insufficient mobility. In duplicated or reduced-suit decks, broadening completion piles to the deck\u2019s natural equivalence classes (such as color instead of exact suit) can convert reversible churn into real progress without rewriting the tableau.\n85. When a reduced deck removes one color entirely, any rule that depends on color alternation can silently eliminate all legal contact. Before tuning difficulty, make sure the deck\u2019s actual suit/color makeup is compatible with every core build rule.\n86. When a solitaire file won\u2019t compile, fix the smallest rule-context mismatch first and preserve the surrounding structure so later playtest data is actually informative. Also, if two move rules share the same source and destination, prefer merging them into one condition tree; overlapping rules often create engine ambiguity without adding meaningful gameplay.\n87. When a solitaire already wins often and uses all of its cards and piles, but some deals are exhausted almost immediately, the next improvement is usually better opening visibility rather than broader move freedom. Increasing initial exposure on a constrained pile type can remove dead starts without turning the whole game into loose storage.\n88. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but zero wins, suspect a sink mismatch before adding more mobility: rearrangement alone rarely converts to victory unless enough cards can leave play irreversibly. Repurposing existing helper spaces into structured progress sinks is often a cleaner fix than adding more generic storage.\n89. When a solitaire variant occasionally wins but most losses exhaust in just a few moves, the main issue is often opening congestion rather than endgame rules. A small redistribution into more, shallower starting piles can improve contact and decision density without making the core move logic much looser.\n90. When a solitaire variant already has some wins but many deals are proven impossible very early, the problem is often not overall mobility but the combination of cramped starts and an overly all-at-once scoring sink. Small setup changes that improve initial contact, paired with a sink that accepts incremental progress, usually raise fairness more effectively than making tableau movement broadly looser.\n91. When a solitaire variant has a separate \u201cprogress area,\u201d make sure it is a true completion sink rather than just another place to park cards. If victory depends on clearing active piles, there must be a legal, irreversible path that can absorb the whole deck in realistic chunks under the game\u2019s actual building rules.\n92. When a game wins consistently but one pile type shows persistent non-use, first check whether you've over-provisioned a sink or helper area relative to the deck's actual completion units. Reallocating redundant piles into constrained workspace can preserve solvability while increasing decision density and making more of the layout matter.\n93. When a solitaire produces long, high-usage losses with no wins, the usual issue is not lack of legal moves but lack of a sink that matches how the deck can actually be completed. Make the irreversible scoring area large enough and relevant enough to absorb the deck, then tune mobility around that structure.\n94. When some deals already win but many others are exhausted almost immediately, the bottleneck is often opening contact rather than the overall objective. A count-preserving redistribution into more, smaller tableau piles can improve fairness across shuffles by exposing more top cards and creating workspace earlier without rewriting the game\u2019s core rules.\n95. When a one-way receiving pile exists, either make it the true completion sink or give it a legal release route; otherwise it becomes dead storage that the solver explores heavily but can never convert into a win.\n\nIf losses are long and non-exhaustive, check for sink/win mismatch before loosening tableau rules. Search churn often comes from reversible motion around a structurally impossible objective.\n96. When a one-way helper pile is not itself part of the victory sink, it needs a reliable release path; otherwise high 