ument to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, rule-slot conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer the smallest count-preserving, role-preserving repair. A runnable near-neighbor reveals more than a speculative redesign layered on top of an invalid file.\n- Re-check setup, move rules, sink rules, and win clauses together after every structural fix. Nonstandard suit/color makeup, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, and engine-specific rule contexts can make familiar-looking rules unreachable or impossible.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine effectively allows one rule per source/destination pair, merge special cases into one condition tree instead of splitting them into competing moves.\n- Treat stock/deal piles according to their actual engine role. A deal-only stock is setup/feeding infrastructure unless the rules explicitly support direct play from it.\n\n## Align irreversible progress, sinks, and victory\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can absorb the entire deck under ordinary play. Rearrangement alone is not a completion plan.\n- Verify both sink capacity and sink legality against the real deck structure. In reduced-suit, duplicated-suit, or multi-copy decks, foundation count, start rules, matching rules, and maximum build length must scale to the actual completion units present.\n- Match the sink to how the game naturally assembles cards. Sinks that accept small, frequent, ordinary increments usually produce real progress; sinks that only reward rare preassembled chunks or perfect runs usually create churn and unwinnability.\n- Align the win condition to the piles that already represent irreversible progress. If a receiving area is the true sink, score completion there instead of requiring unrelated holding piles to empty.\n- In decks with repeated suits or duplicate ranks, standard ace-up exact-suit foundations are often the wrong completion model. Broader sink compatibility or run-based completion can fit the deck\u2019s real completion units better than adding more tableau freedom.\n- Keep one-way destinations purposeful. A pile that only receives cards should usually be part of victory or have a reliable release path; otherwise it becomes dead storage, pseudo-progress, and search noise.\n- Avoid over-provisioned or over-permissive sinks. Extra sink piles beyond the deck\u2019s real completion needs, or sinks that accept almost anything, preserve solvability while collapsing pile roles and decision quality.\n\n## Shape mobility to create progress, not churn\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Very short exhausted losses usually indicate missing completion paths or poor opening contact; long high-usage losses and search-cap failures usually indicate reversible motion disconnected from victory.\n- When mobility is high but wins are absent, fix sink mismatch before adding more freedom. Broadening sink compatibility, repurposing an existing one-way area into the main sink, or changing the rewarded completion pattern is usually higher leverage than loosening tableau rules.\n- Favor movement rules that build in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau and stack moves should help expose and assemble sink-ready structures instead of demanding one pattern in the tableau and a different rare pattern at completion.\n- If some wins already exist but most losses hit search limits, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding more mobility. Empty-pile fills, helper-pile parking, redeals, stock rotation, and oversized stack transfers are common churn sources.\n- Prefer constrained workspace over generic storage. Single-card access, rank-gated empties, or stack rules that mirror ordinary building usually preserve planning value while reducing solver noise.\n- When access is the issue, add structured mobility rather than loose mobility. A stack transfer or local compatibility rule that mirrors the game\u2019s build pattern often improves progress more than adding unrestricted parking or extra generic moves.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small setup changes\n- When losses exhaust in 0\u20134 moves across many shuffles, first check for a real completion path. If the sink is valid, then treat the problem as opening contact.\n- If some deals already win but many others die immediately, prefer small exposure or layout changes over broad rules loosening. More face-up information, more even tableau depths, or one more live pile often improves shuffle fairness without flattening the game.\n- When layouts are fully exposed yet still stall immediately, the bottleneck may be overly specific contact rules rather than hidden information. Loosening tableau compatibility one step can create early branching while keeping the sink strict.\n- Use count-preserving setup changes to tune both contact and pacing. Redistributing the same cards across more or shallower piles can improve exposure, workspace, congestion, and deal rhythm at once.\n```"}]